1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,905
This is an unofficial Session 1 study artifact for Solana School Fall 2026.

2
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:14,342
It covers the execution model, accounts, transactions, parallel scheduling, Program Derived Addresses, cross-program invocation,

3
00:00:14,342 --> 00:00:18,592
compute limits, clusters, and the Anchor development loop.

4
00:00:18,592 --> 00:00:26,559
The material is based on personal notes and checked against public Solana and Anchor documentation.

5
00:00:26,559 --> 00:00:30,809
It is not an official Solana Foundation presentation.

6
00:00:31,609 --> 00:00:39,013
A Solana client chooses a program, supplies instruction data, and declares the accounts the instruction will use.

7
00:00:39,013 --> 00:00:43,803
One or more instructions are placed in a transaction and signed.

8
00:00:43,803 --> 00:00:51,207
The validator loads the target program and the requested accounts, then the SVM executes the program logic.

9
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:57,739
Persistent application state normally lives in separate data accounts rather than in program-local global state.

10
00:00:57,739 --> 00:01:05,143
This separation matters because the runtime knows exactly which state a transaction may access before execution begins.

11
00:01:05,943 --> 00:01:12,037
A persistent account contains lamports, arbitrary data bytes, an owner program ID, an executable

12
00:01:12,037 --> 00:01:15,084
flag, and the deprecated rent epoch field.

13
00:01:15,084 --> 00:01:21,178
The owner field names the program allowed to modify account data or debit lamports.

14
00:01:21,178 --> 00:01:25,966
It does not identify a person who holds a private key.

15
00:01:25,966 --> 00:01:28,578
Signer and writable status are different.

16
00:01:28,578 --> 00:01:31,625
They are privileges declared for an instruction.

17
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:39,025
Signer status means a required signature was supplied, or the runtime granted signer privilege for a PDA.

18
00:01:39,025 --> 00:01:42,942
Writable status means the instruction may modify the account.

19
00:01:42,942 --> 00:01:46,860
These flags are not stored as permanent account fields.

20
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:53,726
An instruction identifies a target program, an ordered list of account metadata, and an

21
00:01:53,726 --> 00:01:56,759
opaque byte array interpreted by that program.

22
00:01:56,759 --> 00:02:03,259
A transaction combines one or more instructions with a recent blockhash and the required signatures.

23
00:02:03,259 --> 00:02:05,859
Transactions are atomic for state changes.

24
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:11,925
If a later instruction fails, changes made by earlier instructions in the same transaction

25
00:02:11,925 --> 00:02:15,825
are rolled back, and later instructions do not run.

26
00:02:15,825 --> 00:02:22,758
The transaction can still be charged its fee because validators performed signature verification and execution work.

27
00:02:23,558 --> 00:02:29,226
Before execution, each transaction declares which accounts are read-only and which are writable.

28
00:02:29,226 --> 00:02:32,715
Multiple transactions may read the same account concurrently.

29
00:02:32,715 --> 00:02:38,819
A transaction that writes an account requires exclusive access to that account, so it

30
00:02:38,819 --> 00:02:43,616
conflicts with other readers or writers until the lock is available.

31
00:02:43,616 --> 00:02:48,412
Transactions whose account sets do not conflict can execute in parallel.

32
00:02:48,412 --> 00:02:51,464
This is why accurate account declarations matter.

33
00:02:51,464 --> 00:02:58,441
Marking an unnecessary account writable grants extra privilege and can reduce concurrency by creating avoidable contention.

34
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:05,749
A Program Derived Address is calculated from application-specific seeds, a program ID, and a bump.

35
00:03:05,749 --> 00:03:11,823
The canonical bump is the first valid value found while searching downward from 255.

36
00:03:11,823 --> 00:03:18,331
The resulting address is off the Ed25519 curve, so it has no corresponding private key.

37
00:03:18,331 --> 00:03:21,802
Deriving the address does not create an account.

38
00:03:21,802 --> 00:03:27,876
A program instruction must still allocate and fund an account at that address if

39
00:03:27,876 --> 00:03:29,611
persistent storage is needed.

40
00:03:29,611 --> 00:03:35,686
During a cross-program invocation, invoke signed lets the runtime grant signer privilege when the

41
00:03:35,686 --> 00:03:40,458
calling program supplies the seeds and bump that reproduce its PDA.

42
00:03:41,258 --> 00:03:45,195
Cross-program invocation allows one Solana program to call another.

43
00:03:45,195 --> 00:03:51,319
Signer and writable privileges can propagate from the caller, but the called program cannot

44
00:03:51,319 --> 00:03:54,819
receive greater privileges than the caller was given.

45
00:03:54,819 --> 00:04:01,380
A program can authorize its PDA by supplying the correct derivation seeds to invoke signed.

46
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:04,442
This does not create a private-key signature.

47
00:04:04,442 --> 00:04:09,254
Account locking should not be described as a general reentrancy guard.

48
00:04:09,254 --> 00:04:12,316
Direct self-recursion is allowed within instruction-stack limits.

49
00:04:12,316 --> 00:04:18,440
Indirect reentrancy, such as program A calling B and B calling A again, is

50
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:20,190
rejected by the runtime.

51
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:24,639
Compute units meter the work performed during execution.

52
00:04:24,639 --> 00:04:31,023
Under the documented defaults, each non-builtin instruction receives 200 thousand compute units, and a

53
00:04:31,023 --> 00:04:34,216
transaction may request up to 1.4 million.

54
00:04:34,216 --> 00:04:40,145
The base transaction fee is 5 thousand lamports per signature. Priority is separate.

55
00:04:40,145 --> 00:04:46,530
The priority fee is the ceiling of the requested compute-unit limit multiplied by the

56
00:04:46,530 --> 00:04:50,178
compute-unit price in micro-lamports, divided by 1 million.

57
00:04:50,178 --> 00:04:56,107
Raising the limit alone does not increase priority when the price is zero.

58
00:04:56,107 --> 00:05:02,492
The requested limit, not actual compute consumption, is used to calculate the priority fee.

59
00:05:03,292 --> 00:05:07,855
Localnet is a private validator used for fast, repeatable development.

60
00:05:07,855 --> 00:05:11,505
Its ledger and local test SOL are disposable.

61
00:05:11,505 --> 00:05:16,524
Devnet is a public development cluster with faucets and Explorer visibility.

62
00:05:16,524 --> 00:05:22,000
Devnet SOL has no monetary value, and cluster state may be reset.

63
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:27,475
Mainnet beta is the production network and uses assets with real value.

64
00:05:27,475 --> 00:05:32,038
Session 1 development and deployment stay on localnet or devnet.

65
00:05:32,038 --> 00:05:39,795
Before diagnosing a cluster-specific problem, run Solana config get and confirm the active RPC endpoint and wallet.

66
00:05:40,595 --> 00:05:46,287
In an Anchor project, the Rust program lives under the programs directory.

67
00:05:46,287 --> 00:05:52,453
Anchor dot toml records provider settings, wallet configuration, test commands, and program IDs.

68
00:05:52,453 --> 00:05:56,247
TypeScript integration tests live under the tests directory.

69
00:05:56,247 --> 00:06:02,414
Anchor build produces the deployable program, an interface description, and generated client types.

70
00:06:02,414 --> 00:06:10,477
Anchor test starts or connects to a validator, deploys the program, and runs the configured test command.

71
00:06:10,477 --> 00:06:14,746
Files under target deploy can include a deployment keypair.

72
00:06:14,746 --> 00:06:20,912
That keypair determines the program ID and must never be committed or published.

73
00:06:21,712 --> 00:06:26,862
A client derives any required addresses and fetches current account state.

74
00:06:26,862 --> 00:06:34,822
It builds instructions, declares account privileges, groups the instructions in a transaction, and obtains the required signatures.

75
00:06:34,822 --> 00:06:40,909
An RPC node receives the transaction and forwards it into the validator network.

76
00:06:40,909 --> 00:06:47,933
The runtime detects account conflicts, acquires the necessary locks, and runs each instruction in order.

77
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:52,147
If every instruction succeeds, the account changes are committed.

78
00:06:52,147 --> 00:06:56,829
If any instruction fails, all state changes are rolled back.

79
00:06:56,829 --> 00:07:03,384
The transaction signature and program logs provide the evidence needed to inspect the result.

80
00:07:04,184 --> 00:07:10,964
When a Solana program fails, check the active tool versions, cluster, wallet, and program

81
00:07:10,964 --> 00:07:12,901
IDs before changing code.

82
00:07:12,901 --> 00:07:17,259
Reproduce the problem locally with Anchor test when possible.

83
00:07:17,259 --> 00:07:21,133
Read the exact transaction error and program logs.

84
00:07:21,133 --> 00:07:27,429
Then check account funding, signer status, writable privileges, PDA seeds, and compute use.

85
00:07:27,429 --> 00:07:34,208
The accompanying repository contains the detailed Session 1 notes, commands, reproducible lab, and links

86
00:07:34,208 --> 00:07:37,598
to the official sources used for verification.
